  (RUNTIME-EXCEPTION.md).
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  yyjson.pcpp - the yyjson unit: `import yyjson;` reads and writes JSON with
  yyjson (https://github.com/ibireme/yyjson), a fast JSON library in C. A
  self-describing vendor module: it owns every include path, library path
  and link library it needs, per official target. The library is
  libyyjson.a (one per target), built by PaskalCPP-Libs' build-yyjson.cmd
  from yyjson's own CMake project (static, the full API at upstream's
  defaults), shipped under yyjson's MIT license.

  The API is yyjson's C API as it ships, called straight from PaskalCPP:
  - Reading: yyjson_read(text, length, flags) parses UTF-8 text into a
    read-only `ptr to yyjson_doc` (nil on failure); yyjson_read_opts takes a
    `ptr to yyjson_read_err` that receives the error code (a
    YYJSON_READ_ERROR_* constant), its message and the byte position in the
    text where reading stopped. yyjson_read_file(path, flags, nil, err)
    reads a file: it opens it with the C runtime's fopen, so with HookIO
    imported it reads a registered IO's io:// path as well, a zip entry of
    bin\assets.zip included, with no extra unit. A failure is told by the
    error's code, not its text: a file that does not open is
    YYJSON_READ_ERROR_FILE_OPEN, whose message yyjson's reader spells
    "failed to read file". yyjson_doc_free frees the document and every
    value in it.
  - Walking: yyjson_doc_get_root gives the root `ptr to yyjson_val`;
    yyjson_obj_get(obj, "key") a member (nil when it is not there),
    yyjson_arr_get(arr, index) / yyjson_arr_size an array's items;
    yyjson_is_obj / _is_arr / _is_str ... test a value's kind and
    yyjson_get_str / _sint / _int / _real / _num / _bool read it (0, false
    or nil for a value of another kind). The strings yyjson_get_str gives
    belong to the document: they live until yyjson_doc_free.
  - Building: yyjson_mut_doc_new(nil) makes a mutable document,
    yyjson_mut_obj / yyjson_mut_arr its containers, yyjson_mut_doc_set_root
    its root; yyjson_mut_obj_add_str / _int / _real / _bool / _val and
    yyjson_mut_arr_add_* add members and items. yyjson_mut_obj_add_str and
    every other add that takes a `ptr to const char` keep the POINTER, not a
    copy: a string literal is fine, the text of a PaskalCPP string that
    changes or ends before the document does is not; yyjson_mut_obj_add_strcpy
    (and yyjson_mut_strcpy for a value) copy the text into the document.
    yyjson_mut_doc_free frees the document.
  - Writing: yyjson_mut_write(doc, flags, nil) / yyjson_write give the text
    (YYJSON_WRITE_PRETTY for indented output); the text is malloc'd by
    yyjson and is freed with free(). yyjson_mut_write_file(path, doc, flags,
    nil, err) writes a file through the C runtime (and so through HookIO).
==============================================================================*/

module unit yyjson;

// Self-describing vendor module: it owns every path it needs.

@addincludepath "$P:res/libs/vendor/yyjson/include";

@ifdef TARGET_X86_64_WINDOWS_GNU
  @addlibrarypath "$P:res/libs/vendor/yyjson/lib/x86_64-windows-gnu";
@elseif TARGET_X86_64_LINUX_GNU
  @addlibrarypath "$P:res/libs/vendor/yyjson/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu";
@else
  @message error "yyjson: unsupported target";
@endif

@linklibrary "yyjson";

// The header goes to this unit's header, so every importer sees yyjson's API
cpp header;

#include "yyjson.h"

end.
